# Extra SITL defaults, layered on top of the frame's stock parameter file.
# Loaded by ./sitl.sh through the SITL --defaults flag (comma-separated list).
#
# Every name here must exist in this firmware -- and NOTHING TELLS YOU IF IT DOESN'T.
# An unknown name is silently skipped: read_param_defaults_file() (AP_Param.cpp) sets
# done_all=false and continues, load_defaults_file() still returns true, and the
# "Ignored unknown param" printf is behind #if ENABLE_DEBUG (off in a normal build).
# The panic only fires if the FILE cannot be opened. Verified: a defaults file holding
# RNGFND_LNADING and THIS_IS_NOT_A_PARAM boots clean.
#
# So a typo here is a silent no-op -- the same failure mode as a stale eeprom value
# below. The only proof a line is in force is reading the live value over MAVLink.
#
# These are DEFAULTS, not values. A parameter already written to
# sitl-run/eeprom.bin -- by QGroundControl, or by an earlier session -- keeps
# its stored value and ignores the line here. So a setting below can silently
# not be in force; ./sitl.sh --wipe resets the EEPROM and makes these win.

# Terrain off by default: a flat sim needs no tiles and no GCS to serve them.
# Run ./sitl.sh --terrain to layer config/sitl-terrain.parm over this and get
# real elevation. TERRAIN_FOLLOW stays off even then -- it makes Plane hold a
# terrain-relative altitude, which is a flight-behaviour change, not a sensor.
#
# An earlier note here claimed terrain does blocking tile I/O inside the
# lockstep physics loop and stalls the sim at high --speedup. That is not what
# the code does: schedule_disk_io() is a non-blocking state machine and the
# read()/write() run on the IO thread (TerrainIO.cpp, register_io_process).
# Measured parked at --speedup=20: 6.9x with terrain, 7.1x without.
TERRAIN_ENABLE 0
TERRAIN_FOLLOW 0

# The simulated downward altimeter, modelled on the Benewake TF03 this airframe
# actually carries: 0.1-180 m. (The real sensor's 0.5 deg beam is NOT modelled --
# SIM_Aircraft::rangefinder_beam_width() is 0, so the sim's beam is a pencil; there
# is no parameter for it.)
#
# Type 100 is the SITL backend (AP_RangeFinder.h Type::SIM); it publishes SITL's
# height_agl. That height is terrain-aware ONLY when TERRAIN_ENABLE=1 and a tile
# covers the vehicle. Otherwise set_height_agl() (AP_HAL_SITL/SITL_State.cpp)
# silently falls back to a flat-earth model and reports height above HOME.
# Nothing warns you: the sensor still tracks your climbs and descents, it just
# never sees a hill. ./sitl.sh --terrain checks for the tile up front.
RNGFND1_TYPE 100
RNGFND1_ORIENT 25

# TF03 range, as AP_Int16 centimetres (these become metre floats in 4.6's
# RNGFND1_MIN/MAX). Past MAX the frontend reports OutOfRangeHigh and Plane falls
# back to the baro -- it does not clamp the value (AP_RangeFinder_Backend.cpp,
# update_status).
RNGFND1_MIN_CM 10
# 18000 is not merely a cutoff. rangefinder_height_update() (ArduPlane/altitude.cpp)
# scales its in-range latch off MAX: it wants 10 samples differing from the first
# by >5% of MAX, and resets the count on a jump >20% of MAX. At the old 32767 those
# gates were 16.4 m and 65.5 m; at the real 180 m they are 9 m and 36 m. Padding MAX
# does not just admit impossible readings, it desensitises the landing latch.
RNGFND1_MAX_CM 18000

# What this actually switches on: rangefinder_correction() gates on (RNGFND_LANDING &&
# flight_stage == LAND), so at the firmware default of 0 the lidar never feeds TECS and
# rangefinder_state.in_use ("Rangefinder engaged") never arms. Setting it to 1 is what
# makes the sensor do its job.
#
# It does NOT gate the sensor being read. rangefinder_height_update() polls every cycle
# and populates rangefinder_state.correction whenever the lidar is merely in_range --
# no RNGFND_LANDING check. And adjust_landing_slope_for_rangefinder_bump()
# (AP_Landing_Slope.cpp, called from mode.cpp on every landing approach) reads that
# .correction DIRECTLY, guarded only by LAND_SLOPE_RCALC (default 2.0, not 0). So even
# at RNGFND_LANDING=0 an in-range lidar can already re-cut the glide slope. Do not read
# "0" as "the rangefinder is inert".
#
# "Landing only" is true for FIXED-WING Plane. It is not the whole story on a QUADPLANE,
# which is this branch's default frame: relative_ground_altitude() (altitude.cpp:116)
# hands back the lidar height whenever (rangefinder_landing && in_range), with no
# flight-stage gate. Most of its callers sit behind params that are 0 here
# (Q_ASSIST_ALT, Q_FWD_THR_USE, Q_VFWD_GAIN, Q_WVANE_HGT_MIN, LGR_ENABLE) -- but
# update_throttle_suppression() (quadplane.cpp:1965) has NO enabling param and runs
# from motors_output() in every VTOL mode. So on a quadplane, RNGFND_LANDING 1 also
# switches that motor-suppression height check from baro to the lidar.
#
# A stored eeprom.bin value beats this default -- ./sitl.sh says so, --wipe fixes it.
RNGFND_LANDING 1

# Deliberately NOT set: SIM_SONAR_RND, the sensor noise amplitude (default 0). The
# TF03's real error -- +/-10 cm close in, ~1% (1.8 m) at 180 m -- is dwarfed by the
# ~6% baro bias in SITL-CHEATSHEET.md, and a deterministic sim is worth more than
# cosmetic jitter.
#
# SIM_SONAR_GLITCH cannot help here either -- but NOT because it does nothing. It is
# read (AP_HAL_SITL/sitl_rangefinder.cpp, _sonar_pin_voltage) by the ANALOG rangefinder
# path, where it forces a max-distance reading with the given probability. RNGFND1_TYPE
# 100 never goes through that path: Type::SIM reads rangefinder_m[] straight from
# Aircraft::rangefinder_range(), which consults sonar_noise and not sonar_glitch. So
# setting it has no effect on THIS config, and glitching would have to be written.

# Log from boot rather than from arming, so a session that never arms still
# leaves a .bin behind under sitl-run/logs/.
LOG_DISARMED 1

# Deliberately NOT set: SIM_FLOAT_EXCEPT.
# On macOS this build never arms the FPU trap-enable bits at all -- see the
# __APPLE__ branch of AP_HAL_SITL/Scheduler.cpp -- so the param has no effect
# here. On Linux its default of 1 catches real FP bugs and is worth keeping.
